@1 = ~Annaebiel claims a priest placed a curse on Coran that will kill him if he leaves her. We need to find a temple of Helm nearby and ask for help.~
@2 = ~Annaebiel lied about the curse and released Coran. The affair is over.~
@3 = ~Coran left Annaebiel behind. Whatever their past was, it is now at an end.~
@4 = ~Priest of Helm from Baldur's Gate made it clear that Coran does not suffer from curse or any other magical affliction. It is likely just a matter of a dispute between two former lovers. Coran wants to confront Annaebiel about her machinations.~
@5 = ~Hello. Are you Selamiel? Coran Selamiel?~
@6 = ~Ehm. No...~
@7 = ~Then I do not understand what you are doing in my house.~
@8 = ~Hello. Are you Selamiel? Coran Selamiel?~
@9 = ~Coran! You lowlife, lousy fool!~
@10 = ~I wrote you tons of letters! I waited, and waited, for Tymora knows how long! Where have you been?! Why did you leave me like this?!~
@11 = ~Yes! Letters! The address you gave me last time... was that even a proper one?~
@12 = ~Coran! You don't have a brother! You're an only child! You promised me we would be together forever! Was I stupid to listen to you?~
@13 = ~Annathela?!~
@14 = ~Your empty promises meant nothing, then! You can't even remember my name?! The name of the person you claimed you loved most?~
@15 = ~WHAT?! You and Ar'leya?! You swine! I should've listened to my mother!~
@16 = ~You know what, Coran? I forgive you.~
@17 = ~I love you, Coran. And I believe we can rekindle an old flame.~
@18 = ~Yes, you foolish elf. As per our promise! The one you made back when you fed me those lies about how I don't even compare to the other women you've met.~
@19 = ~Coran is naught but mine, and this is our fate. The stars have aligned for us, my love! We are one!~
@20 = ~Your new friend understands, and soon you will too. Fate is a powerful tool, and accepting it often comes with a price.~
@21 = ~My love, there is no need for you to go alone! I'll come with you! I'll always be with you now. Every day, every night, every second. For the rest of your life.~
@22 = ~I even had a priest cast a curse on you so that you die a painful, agonising death if you leave me again.~
@23 = ~I know, right? The things love can make you do. It came into fruition as soon as you stepped through the door. The curse was activated by my presence near you. Don't you think this is sweet?~
@24 = ~I don't wish you dead, silly. I wish you alive, well and with me. Think about it. You and I, cuddled up together in a warm bed, by the fireplace. Day and night. We can make baby Corans running around and...~
@25 = ~You? You are... nothing! Coran. You have the audacity to abandon me for a mongrel like this? You are not worthy of me. I thought we shared a special bond. I... cursed you with the help of a local preacher.~
@26 = ~You again? Do you really think calling Coran yours will change anything? He already knows what I did to him. The curse is real, and your lie will not undo it.~
@27 = ~The moment you walked in through the door, you've become marked with a magical spell. Being in my proximity made the curse activate. Walking away will kill you.~
@28 = ~Everything was going to be different. You were supposed to be my love. In darkness and in light. In joy and sadness. Your words now... they hurt me beyond repair. You left me and got with another woman. This isn't real.~
@29 = ~If you must die, so be it. Walk away. Die an agonising death, like you deserve, you deceitful, manipulative elf.~
@30 = ~We'll find a priest so he can undo the curse. Let's go, Coran.~
@31 = ~I'm sorry, Coran. Seems like you are stuck with her. I think we should part ways here. Can't have your death on my conscience.~
@32 = ~You think I'd give him up because he and I are different? Opposites attract and create tension, thrill and desire. A woman of your standards wouldn't understand. I can tell you are all over him just by the spark in your eyes but... you don't compare to me.~
@33 = ~Coran is our partner in crime. Not literally! He is just... he's... mine... I mean ours. Ehm...~
@34 = ~Coran. I feel your frustration, but this is your personal business. I'd be nosy if I intervened.~
@35 = ~Coran is mine. (lie)~
@36 = ~Eldoth. As much as it pains me to say it, you are a philanderer who knows how to appease a woman.~
@37 = ~You'll do no such thing! Our destinies are entwined now, and you shall do as I command!~
@38 = ~Stay back, woman! He's coming with us.~
@39 = ~One more step and you are dead.~
@40 = ~Coran is mine. (lie)~
@41 = ~Safana? Try talking some sense into her.~
@42 = ~Eldoth? Perhaps you can reason with her.~
@43 = ~You'll do no such thing! Our destinies are entwined now, and you shall do as I command!~
@44 = ~Stay back, woman! He's coming with us.~
@45 = ~One more step and you are dead.~
@46 = ~Coran is mine. (lie)~
@47 = ~Safana? Try talking some sense into her.~
@48 = ~Eldoth? Perhaps you can reason with her.~
@49 = ~Speaking like someone who knows how to treat a woman. You are good with wordplay, bard... and quite charming.~
@50 = ~(she blushes) Oh, Eldoth. I am... flattered that I have garnered your attention. No one, save for Coran, has ever spoken to me like that. I wanted to rekindle an old flame, using nothing but my own inflated need for affection to win an old love back but... I should simply find a man who shares my interests and openly proves his love to me. Not the one who leaves with no announcement.~
@51 = ~Coran! There is no curse. I lied because I wanted to keep you here... potentially frighten you if things go awry. You're free to go. My actions were... selfish. Not as selfish as you abandoning me, that is.~
@52 = ~Eeeh... sure. It's forgiven. Just... don't show your face in my house again, Coran.~
@53 = ~Farewell now.~
@54 = ~So you do intend to leave, despite knowing what awaits you. Very well. Go find your Helmite. But do not mistake my willingness to let you walk away for forgiveness.~
@55 = ~Then deal with it. I have said what I needed to say, and Coran knows the price of leaving me.~
@56 = ~Look, <CHARNAME>. I'm sought-after. Ladies from these parts recognise the cavalier who brings the thrill and the charm. Whom do I have the pleasure of seeing?~
@57 = ~Ehm... although, at times, fame has its cons.~
@58 = ~Letters? Ehm...~
@59 = ~Ehm... eh... well... I moved out. I forgot to let you know... and then my brother... died and... oh, how tragic it was. I had to be with him in his final moments, and...~
@60 = ~Annathela! I...~
@61 = ~Forgive me, Florencia. No! I meant Florinna? Flora?~
@62 = ~<CHARNAME>, next time let's abstain from entering other people's houses, yeah? Or else we're going to meet her cousin, whom I also had a fling with.~
@63 = ~No. I was just thinking out loud, Annastaciella!~
@64 = ~You want... what? You'd want us to be back together?~
@65 = ~I... I... ehm... <CHARNAME>?~
@66 = ~Coran is our partner in crime. Not literally! He is just... he's... mine... I mean ours. Ehm...~
@67 = ~Coran. I feel your frustration, but this is your personal business. I'd be nosy if I intervened.~
@68 = ~Coran is mine. (lie)~
@69 = ~Safana? He might need your unusually persuasive charisma.~
@70 = ~Eldoth. As much as it pains me to say it, you are a philanderer who knows how to appease a woman.~
@71 = ~(yelp) I am sure we can consummate our love, alas, first I need to pack my things and... move in here? With you?~
@72 = ~What?!~
@73 = ~Analia... love is a tender feeling, not wishing for your loved one's death if they stray an inch off the beaten path.~
@74 = ~(sigh) You had it, Coran. You lived a trivial, jolly life, and it caught up with you.~
@75 = ~You can't just put a curse on someone out of love! Do you realise what you did?~
@76 = ~Have no fear, Coran. We can find this priest and undo the curse.~
@77 = ~I'm sorry, Coran. Seems like you are stuck with her. I think we should part ways here. Can't have your death on my conscience.~
@78 = ~If we find the priest who did it, they can undo the curse.~
@79 = ~That stands to reason. I am not familiar with curses, but a Helmite might be. We should hurry to a Helm temple immediately.~
@80 = ~Settle down, my friend. No need for hostility. We are all friends here. You already know about the curse, and I would rather not make this uglier than it needs to be.~
@81 = ~That lie changes nothing, <CHARNAME>. I already know the curse is real. Let's stop playing games and deal with what comes next.~
@82 = ~I struggle to perceive if you're doing this out of worry about my well-being or quite the opposite. I'll carry that weight on my own, then. I am sorry to let you down, <CHARNAME>.~
@83 = ~You did what?~
@84 = ~What?!!~
@85 = ~Yeah, we better hurry.~
@86 = ~You can't blame an elf for craving an adventurous life. For all it's worth, I am sorry for leaving you, Cecilia.~
@87 = ~You have my vow.~
@88 = ~You're on your own with that one, elf. If you hadn't acted like a scoundrel, maybe you wouldn't end up in these dire circumstances.~
@89 = ~My h-heart goes out to you, Coran. If I ever let Jaheira wait for longer than a few hours, she w-would be fuming.~
@90 = ~Casting a s-s-spell to keep your loved one near you?~
@91 = ~Her blood is boiling right now, Coran. Trust me, there is nothing more dangerous than a furious woman. Not even being surrounded by devils in the Nine Hells is worse. I strongly advise you to refrain before you cause more damage.~
@92 = ~Oh, maybe I was misdirected.~
@93 = ~I was VERY misdirected.~
@94 = ~Interesting. You've been saying the same thing to me.~
@95 = ~I can curry a little favour with her. Listen, lady. I am not sure what kind of flame you wish to rekindle with Coran, but he is a womanizer with a reputation beyond his means. His way of life is... extravagant, to put it mildly. You're keen to cling to him like a moth to a torchlight, yet his lifestyle and cravings do not match yours. Woman to woman, none other can give you a better answer.~
@96 = ~I openly express pity over the humiliation you must feel, Coran.~
@97 = ~Coran may be a fool, but a curse is no way to keep a man you love. Let him choose what he wants for himself, Annaebiel.~
@98 = ~Wilder love story I have never seen. I am torn between finding it bittersweet romantic or terribly over-the-top nonsensical.~
@99 = ~It was just itching me to say how pathetic the unfolding scene is. Women are meant to be strong, independent, and not reliant on men, yet she gave her heart and cast a spell on an estranged ex-lover. Painful to admit, but I... side with the elf on this one. Her mentality has been botched.~
@100 = ~Coran, think of the positives. This is sort of romantic. I wouldn't mind if the love of my life did that to me one day.~
@101 = ~Blind love is capable of dealing atrocious forms of damage. I hope she is bluffing about the curse.~
@102 = ~Purely ironic. An unapologetic philanderer and an unstable half-elven nitwit. If there's any doubt why drow society, despite its downsides, stays functional, it's because we waste no effort on feelings such as love or emotion. Power is a surgical tool, whereas love is a weakness that clouds your mind. This one succumbed to it. Shar looks at you with shame.~
@103 = ~I would totally say I am happy to see a love story unfold, but my opinion drastically changed when I heard 'curse'.~
@104 = ~Voyaging across half the Sword Coast with this bunch of simpletons, and where has it got me? Love drama. This is beneath a Red Wizard of Thay to engage in personal affairs between two absolute dregs of society. (To think that I, Edwin Odesseiron, had to join forces with these simians.)~
@105 = ~'Scuse me, M'lady. Name's Eldoth Cron. It is unusually lovely to meet a woman of vestiges. You cling to the past, to the man who once shared your feelings. Such a snowflaked angel wasting away all her love on rubble like this. Love is freedom, a salve for one's soul. You shouldn't be plagued with nonsensical whims of ancient history and shape your life in accordance with your current goals and desires.~
@106 = ~I would hate to take the choice away from you, but if you allow me the honour of restoring my friend here to full health, maybe you and I can spend some time together. Berduskan wine, atmospheric lighting, and a massage. I recommend my skills for such a romantic occasion.~
@107 = ~You owe me a pint for that, Coran. But first, I have a meeting with a lovely lady.~
@108 = ~My lady, you are clinging to an old love with more force than any man could survive. Perhaps you should let him choose his own fate before your affection becomes something neither of you can endure.~
@109 = ~Of course not, my love. This was a trick to rescue our companion. Love between you and I is too real to be dispersed and forsaken.~
@110 = ~You aren't actually entertaining the idea of setting up a date with this lunatic woman, are you?~
@111 = ~Oh, Eldoth. You are too charming. I knew you wouldn't leave me.~
@112 = ~You're back. Did you change your mind?~
@113 = ~You remember my name now. That's a start. As for your accusations…~
@114 = ~Coran… I...~
@115 = ~Coran… you sounded just as romantic as the night we met. You know how to seduce a woman, but I see the other side of this coin. You are irresponsible, childish, bratty and somewhat incompetent. Mistake, however… is mine. I shouldn't take your word and chase you inexorably after simple encounter we had. I was head over heels and I always had this gut feeling you'll be back any day now. When a bunch of strangers came in, I was desperately trying to spot you amongst them, and there you were. I went too far with faking the curse. Will you forgive me?~
@116 = ~Yeah, I see what you mean. Let that be a lesson for me. Safe travels, Coran. I... won't forget this.~
@117 = ~Farewell. Watch yourselves.~
@118 = ~Farewell. Watch yourselves.~
@119 = ~I saw through your lies, Annaebiel!~
@120 = ~Save your breath. It'll keep you from spilling more filth. I am a man of enormous patience, but this has gone too far. Attempting to keep me here would cause me discontent, and misery. Is that what you want the love of your life to feel?~
@121 = ~Annaebiel. I admit. Honour has never been a garment that fit me particularly well. My heart is a wild thing, ever chasing the thrill of a new smile, the intoxicating warmth of a new romance. To settle down was simply not my calling. My life is a dance of peril, adventure, and sweet opportunity. I never made a vow we'd be together. What we shared was a beautiful fling—a fleeting, brilliant moment of happiness in a dangerous world. I truly hoped you would cherish it for what it was, just as I always will.~
@122 = ~Yes, Annaebiel. I will. You have my gratitude for confessing, and we shall leave it at that. Next time, choose your lovers more carefully and don't let yourself be discouraged by failures. Failures are fire that fuels your determination to get what you want, in the end.~
@123 = ~Neither will I, whether that's good or not.~
@124 = ~I am.~
@125 = ~What is?~
@126 = ~How could that be? Annaebiel… lied? To keep me by her side?~
@127 = ~Oh, she and I shall have a word.~
@128 = ~Love curse? No respecting priest of Helm would ever cast such petty and unholy spell, no matter the material goods offered in exchange. Standard procedures provided by Helm clergy are healing services. We do not condone or abide by any blunt request of any receivers if they breach our rules and regulations.~
@129 = ~I guarantee you, no priests within the city walls would cast such a curse. Which one of you is afflicted by this curse?~
@130 = ~Allow me to detect and potentially remove the curse.~
@131 = ~Hmm. That is… interesting.~
@132 = ~You are consumed by no curse or any other magical bounds. You're absolutely free and untouched by magic.~
@133 = ~It looks like a matter of a broken heart rather than insolent lover casting a devious spell. I'm glad I dispelled your fears, good elf. Perhaps you should parley with your nemesis and allow her to understand the unlawfulness of her ways.~
@134 = ~We came here on the behalf of our friend. We've been informed that a priest of Helm have cast a love curse on him and we would like to lift it.~
